<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Hand-trimmed fixture standing in for WindowsClient-10-3.6, the latest revision PowerStig ships
  of the Windows 10 STIG. Rule ids, field values and dscresourcemodule attributes are copied from
  the real processed data in the local PowerStig clone rather than invented, so this doubles as
  the field-shape survey #99's map (#98) asked for: every one of the twelve rule types
  WindowsClient-10 carries gets a real rule here, trimmed the way the WindowsServer-2022-DC/-MS
  and WindowsServer-2025-DC/-MS fixtures are (short Description, no VulnDiscussion/RawString
  prose).

  V-220732 (Service) and V-220956 (UserRight) are the two shapes the survey found the existing
  generators disagreeing with before #100 and #101 fixed them: Service's real ServiceState is
  Stopped, not the generator's old hardcoded "started"; UserRight's real Identity is blank, not
  the literal NULL the generator used to require. Both convert correctly now.

  V-220703.a/.b is a real sub-rule pair sharing one registry Key, neither half an organisation
  value - the plainest version of the paired shape WindowsServer-2025's V-278090.a/.b also
  exercises. V-220718.a's real dscresource attribute is WindowsOptionalFeature, not WindowsFeature
  (WindowsClient-11's own WindowsFeature rule carries WindowsFeature instead); dispatch keys on
  the rule-type element, not this attribute, so the variance is harmless - the same conclusion
  Registry's RegistryPolicyFile-vs-Registry variance reached in the WindowsServer fixtures.
-->
